Overview
This Java program implements the Merge Sort algorithm, a divide-and-conquer sorting technique that recursively splits an array into smaller subarrays, sorts them, and then merges them back together.

Code Structure
1. Merge Class (Core Algorithm)
The Merge class contains the two main methods that implement the merge sort algorithm:

Divide(int array[], int start, int end)

Purpose: Recursively divides the array into halves until single elements remain
Parameters:
array: The array to be sorted
start: Starting index of current subarray
end: Ending index of current subarray

Key Operations:
Base case: Returns if subarray has 0 or 1 elements (start >= end)
Calculates midpoint: mid = start + (end-start)/2
Recursively divides left and right halves
Calls Conquer() to merge sorted halves
Conquer(int array[], int start, int mid, int end)

Purpose: Merges two sorted subarrays into a single sorted array
Parameters:
array: Original array being sorted
start: Start index of first subarray
mid: End index of first subarray (mid+1 starts second subarray)
end: End index of second subarray

Key Operations:
Creates temporary array NewArray to hold merged result
Uses three while loops to:
Merge by comparing elements from both subarrays
Copy remaining elements from first subarray (if any)
Copy remaining elements from second subarray (if any)
Copies merged result back to original array

2. Merge_Sort Class (Driver)
The Merge_Sort class contains the main() method that handles user interaction and demonstrates the sorting:

Input Collection:
Prompts user for number of elements to sort
Collects array elements from user input

Sorting Demonstration:
Displays unsorted array
Creates Merge object and calls Divide() to sort the array
Displays sorted array

How Merge Sort Works
Divide Phase:
The array is recursively split into halves until each subarray contains a single element
Conquer (Merge) Phase:
Sorted subarrays are merged by:
Comparing elements from both subarrays
Placing the smaller element in the correct position
Handling any remaining elements
The merged results are copied back to the original array

Time Complexity
Best Case: O(n log n)
Average Case: O(n log n)
Worst Case: O(n log n)

Space Complexity
O(n) - Requires additional space for temporary arrays during merging

Usage Example
Number Of Element You Want to Sort: 5
Input Elements In An Array:---
Enter 1 Element: 12
Enter 2 Element: 5
Enter 3 Element: 8
Enter 4 Element: 19
Enter 5 Element: 3

UnSorted Data Are---
 12 5 8 19 3

Data Are Sorted Using Merge Sort
 3 5 8 12 19

Key Features
Recursive implementation of divide-and-conquer strategy
Stable sort (maintains relative order of equal elements)
Not an in-place sort (requires additional memory)
Well-suited for large datasets

Limitations
Requires O(n) additional space
For small arrays, simpler algorithms like insertion sort might be more efficient
Recursive implementation may cause stack overflow for extremely large arrays

This implementation provides a clear demonstration of the merge sort algorithm with user interaction for input and output display.

